## Summary

`format.bold` takes `{"value"?: bool}`, so a script can set it on or off. The four Line and Page Breaks commands take only `{}` and toggle:

```
$ wordcraft-cli commands --json
{"id":"format.bold", ..., "params":"{\"value\"?: bool}"}
{"id":"para.keepNext", ..., "params":"{}"}
{"id":"para.keepLines", ..., "params":"{}"}
{"id":"para.pageBreakBefore", ..., "params":"{}"}
{"id":"para.widowControl", ..., "params":"{}"}
```

Paragraph properties carry over to the next paragraph on `text.newParagraph`. A script that wants keep with next on a heading only has to remember the state and toggle it off again on the following paragraph. Our generated document ended up with 47 `<w:keepNext/>` and 113 `<w:keepNext w:val="0"/>`.

## Suggestion

Accept an optional `{"value": bool}` on these four commands, the same as `format.bold`. Without `value` they keep toggling, so the ribbon and existing scripts behave the same as today.

## Summary

Agile encrypted `.docx` packages (ECMA-376 / [MS-OFFCRYPTO] §2.3.4.4) can be changed and still open with the correct password. `EncryptionInfo` is stored in the clear, and decrypt treats `dataIntegrity` as optional. Removing that element turns off the HMAC check. AES-CBC then has no authenticity check, so the decrypted package can differ from what was encrypted.

Confidentiality holds: the password verifier still runs, and a wrong password does not decrypt the package. This is an integrity gap, including for files WordCraft itself writes.

Seen on `main` at `fd0bb2fb1ec081e3eddc4d01917637fef0cafdfe`. No modified file was built and the decrypt path was not executed for this report.

## What the code does

`encrypt` always writes a `dataIntegrity` element ([crates/docx/src/crypt.rs](https://github.com/storytold/wordcraft/blob/fd0bb2fb1ec081e3eddc4d01917637fef0cafdfe/crates/docx/src/crypt.rs#L162-L178)).

`decrypt` checks the HMAC only when that element is present, then decrypts the package either way:

```rust
if let Some((enc_key, enc_value)) = &info.integrity {
    // ... HMAC ...
    if !same(&info.hash.hmac(hmac_key, &package)?, expected) {
        return Err(bad("the document is damaged (its integrity check failed)"));
    }
}
```

([crates/docx/src/crypt.rs](https://github.com/storytold/wordcraft/blob/fd0bb2fb1ec081e3eddc4d01917637fef0cafdfe/crates/docx/src/crypt.rs#L95-L106))

The parser maps a missing element to `None` ([same file, lines 655–658](https://github.com/storytold/wordcraft/blob/fd0bb2fb1ec081e3eddc4d01917637fef0cafdfe/crates/docx/src/crypt.rs#L655-L658)). A hostile-input test renames `<dataIntegrity` away and expects `decrypt` to succeed:

```rust
// Data integrity is optional; without it a good file still opens.
cases.push((with("<dataIntegrity", "<nothing"), package.clone(), false));
```

([same file, lines 958–959](https://github.com/storytold/wordcraft/blob/fd0bb2fb1ec081e3eddc4d01917637fef0cafdfe/crates/docx/src/crypt.rs#L958-L959)…

