## What happens today

[`odt::export`](https://github.com/storytold/wordcraft/blob/eae2421/crates/formats/src/odt.rs#L819-L837) first lowers the document to the exchange `Flow` (`model::from_doc_tracked`) and writes ODT from that. `Flow` is the same reduced model the Markdown, HTML and RTF writers use, so everything it doesn't carry is lost on save:

- named paragraph and character styles: the writer emits its own built-in set (default font, Heading 1–6), not the document's styles and their inheritance;
- page setup: one page layout taken from `last_section`, so a document with a portrait body and a landscape section saves as all-landscape;
- headers and footers: none are written, neither first-page nor default;
- anchored text boxes and shapes (a header text box containing a table, for example) are dropped together with their contents;
- fields: PAGE, NUMPAGES, PAGEREF and the TOC are written as their cached text;
- list styles: numbering definitions (levels, formats like `%1.%2.`, indents, restarts) become generic lists;
- table borders, merged cells and rotated cell text.

A quick way to see it: open any .docx with a different first-page header, a landscape section and a numbered heading scheme, save as .odt and open it in LibreOffice.

## Proposal

Write ODT from the full `wordcraft_doc::Document` instead of `Flow`, the way the DOCX writer does. Sketch of the mapping:

| Model | ODF |
|---|---|
| styles with `based_on` | `styles.xml` common styles with `style:parent-style-name`; direct formatting as automatic styles |
| sections | one `style:page-layout` + `style:master-page` per section; the section's first paragraph carries `style:master-page-name`; first-page headers through `style:header-first` |
| headers / footers | `style:header` / `style:footer` (+ `-first`) inside the master page |
| anchored text boxes | `draw:frame` + `draw:text-box`, anchored to page or paragraph |
